    },
    {
      "id": "XvXbtRC3VKxA08OgUzIqs",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 311.18044296874996,
      "y": 794.9999999999999,
      "width": 853.3994140625,
      "height": 525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "dashed",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "at",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1157863615,
      "version": 1563,
      "versionNonce": 387642634,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793280861655,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The idea in one sentence: the `if i < n` check we have had since Stage 0 is a\ndivergence, but it only ever bites one warp, the one that stradles the end of the data.\n\nThreads launched must be a multiple of block size; the data size can be\nanything. The gap is filled with extra threads that must be switched off:\nthat is what the if (i < n) check does.\n\n1003 elements, block size 64: 16 blocks = 1024 threads, 21 extra, all in block 15.\n16 blocks = 32 warps.\n\nOnly the warp that straddles the end of the data (warp 31, threads 992-1023) has\nboth yes and no answers, so only that warp diverges. The other 31 warps are \nall-yes and run at full speed.\n\nThe bigger the data, the smaller the share of diverging warps: this divergence\nis normal and cheap.\n\nThe performance impact of boundary condition control divergence decreases as\nthe size of vectors being processed increased. For a vector of 100, one \nof the four warps will have control divergence, but for a vector of length\n10,000, only one of 313 will have control divergence.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The idea in one sentence: the `if i < n` check we have had since Stage 0 is a\ndivergence, but it only ever bites one warp, the one that stradles the end of the data.\n\nThreads launched must be a multiple of block size; the data size can be\nanything. The gap is filled with extra threads that must be switched off:\nthat is what the if (i < n) check does.\n\n1003 elements, block size 64: 16 blocks = 1024 threads, 21 extra, all in block 15.\n16 blocks = 32 warps.\n\nOnly the warp that straddles the end of the data (warp 31, threads 992-1023) has\nboth yes and no answers, so only that warp diverges. The other 31 warps are \nall-yes and run at full speed.\n\nThe bigger the data, the smaller the share of diverging warps: this divergence\nis normal and cheap.\n\nThe performance impact of boundary condition control divergence decreases as\nthe size of vectors being processed increased. For a vector of 100, one \nof the four warps will have control divergence, but for a vector of length\n10,000, only one of 313 will have control divergence.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Gi4NM4VOxYEZi-x6p_8ND",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1228.4102172851562,
      "y": 760.0000002222223,
      "width": 703.1795654296875,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffec99",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "au",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1743171857,
      "version": 523,
      "versionNonce": 368828113,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793193954763,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "When we say \"threads need to be a multiple of block size\" we mean that\nwe don't launch threads one by one; we launch whole blocks. The\nlaunch is \"16 blocks of 64\" never \"1003 threads.\" So the\nthread count is always blocks x block size, which is always\na multiple of block size.\n\nThe data doesn't care about that; a vector can have\n1003 elements. So you round the block count up, get\na few threads too many, and switch the extras\noff with the check.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "When we say \"threads need to be a multiple of block size\" we mean that\nwe don't launch threads one by one; we launch whole blocks. The\nlaunch is \"16 blocks of 64\" never \"1003 threads.\" So the\nthread count is always blocks x block size, which is always\na multiple of block size.\n\nThe data doesn't care about that; a vector can have\n1003 elements. So you round the block count up, get\na few threads too many, and switch the extras\noff with the check.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
